[41.484954, "o", "  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ `ensurepip` didn't actually restore the files. Let me check if there's a …\r\n[approval] bash: find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"pip*\" -type f 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"pip*\" -type f 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: find /usr/local -name \"pip*.whl\" -o -name \"pip*.tar.gz\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find /usr/local -name \"pip*.whl\" -o -name \"pip*.tar.gz\" 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -c \"import ensurepip; print(ensurepip._bundled)\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -c \"import ensurepip; print(ensurepip._bundled)\" 2>&1\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ There's a bundled pip wheel. `ensurepip` should extract it but it seems t…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 "]
[41.485166, "o", "— approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick smoke test to confirm packages can actually be installe…\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\nEverything is working. Here's what was wrong and what I did:\r\n\r\n**Problem:** The `pip` package directory (`/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/`) was missing from your Python installation. The `pip-24.3.1.d"]
[41.485234, "o", "ist-info` metadata folder was still present (making `ensurepip` think pip was already installed), but the actual module files were gone — so `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` both crashed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** I removed the stale `.dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which extracted the bundled pip wheel (`pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`) back into site-packages. Both `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` now work correctly.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=20 surface=7ba6c5594c80\r\n"]
